  "note": "One Hebrew verb holds the reign and counsel uses: m.cd.ae and m.ce.aa match the same occurrences, so only m.cd.ae is assigned. Its support count cannot distinguish those meanings. Neh 5:7 remains explicitly uncertain: the local translation reads “my heart reigned over me”, while counsel-language has a strong Aramaic and Akkadian comparison. No historical derivation from ruling to advising or promising is asserted. מְלֶכֶת is a common title, so it is included; מֹלֶךְ, מִלְכֹּם, personal names, and the Malkielite gentilic are left for name entries. The Syriac king/queen lexeme is one SEDRA row, but the feminine word is displayed separately. Malchus (SEDRA 1780) is a proper name. Cognates of counsel are deliberately not assigned to reign, and conversely."
